## Likely Exam Questions

### 1. What is the first thing to verify in a phishing email?
**Answer:** Verify the real sender address, not just the display name. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 2. Why are URL shorteners dangerous in phishing analysis?
**Answer:** They hide the final destination and make malicious links harder to identify. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 3. What is a tracking pixel?
**Answer:** A hidden image embedded in an email that alerts the attacker when the email is opened. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 4. Why is BCC suspicious in phishing emails?
**Answer:** It may indicate a mass phishing campaign where recipients are hidden. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 5. What is credential harvesting?
**Answer:** A phishing technique that tricks victims into entering usernames and passwords into a fake login page. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 6. Why is a `.dot` attachment suspicious in a purchase email?
**Answer:** A Word template does not fit the expected context of a receipt or purchase confirmation. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 7. What does `regasms.exe` represent in the exercise?
**Answer:** A malicious executable payload delivered through the phishing chain. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

### 8. Why are multi-stage redirects effective for attackers?
**Answer:** They hide the final phishing page behind trusted-looking intermediary pages and branding. [Source](https://www.genspark.ai/api/files/s/XGYe0Whz)

